  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 259 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe upper-level trough that will help bring precipitation across the \narea today and Saturday will finally depart the region to the east \nSunday, leading to a period of dry conditions. Surface high pressure \nbuilding across the area, along with upper-level ridging over the \nIntermountain West region protruding from a large upper-level high \nover the southwestern CONUS, will support more quiescent conditions \nthrough Tuesday, with seasonal temperatures (highs in the lower 80s) \nfor Sunday through Tuesday.\n\nIn terms of active weather, it appears mainly Wednesday/Thursday \nwill be the time to watch as the upper-level ridging breaks down, \nallowing a mid-level trough to approach the region. An attendant \nsurface cold front is also progged to accompany the trough, although \nthere are significant timing differences among the global models of \nthe front, and thus, timing of precipitation with that boundary. \nSevere weather potential with this front appears to be pretty low at \nthis time, given modest values of instability and shear (25-35 knots \nfor 0-6 km shear magnitudes). The various machine learning severe \nweather probability guidance supports the more limited severe \nweather signal.",
